WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.220
In this video, you review the SUSE Observability agent add-on, which connects a SUSE Virtualization cluster to a SUSE Observability server for centralized monitoring.

00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:19.200
On the Add-ons page, the experimental suse-observability-agent add-on is enabled, and its state is Deploy Successful.

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:22.630
Open the add-on to see its details.

00:00:22.630 --> 00:00:29.440
The add-on installs the SUSE Observability agents in the suse-observability namespace.

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:35.210
Select Show Configuration to view the add-on resource as YAML.

00:00:35.210 --> 00:00:43.830
The configuration opens in a side panel. The last applied configuration shows the add-on as it was first created.

00:00:43.830 --> 00:00:51.160
It installs the suse-observability-agent chart from the SUSE Observability Helm repository.

00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:57.570
Its values start with three empty fields, apiKey, cluster name, and url.

00:00:57.570 --> 00:01:06.850
With Edit YAML, you fill them with the service token, a cluster name, and the receiver URL from SUSE Observability.

00:01:06.850 --> 00:01:13.580
This cluster is already connected, so close the panel without changes.

00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:24.235
The agent add-on is enabled and deployed, and it reports this cluster to SUSE Observability. Next, you create a VPC NAT gateway with Kube-OVN.
